## 8. What this does NOT prove

1. My matrix checks reuse the fixer's generated trees for the diff comparison
   (fresh post/reverse/attack runs are mine); I did not re-run all 8 fixtures
   × 3 phases myself.
2. All green/red is compile-level; nothing was executed.
3. Attack 2 being pre-existing means the fix is not guilty — but also that the
   "systematic" fix leaves a sibling defect class alive and reachable.
4. I did not test the superclass-chain walk (#25) or the two-enums-one-module
   AND owners-same-module corner (both map families colliding at once).
5. The broader corpus (`bun run test`) was not run by anyone; regression
   exposure outside the 8+2 fixtures is unmeasured.

**Bottom line.** Lineage, anchors, reverse test, matrix and custody: all check
out. The fix is a correct repair for the class-collision axis it targets. But
its safety story for the enum-module axis is falsified: a natural shape (two
payload enums in one module) compiles red (2×E0432) at the fixed state through
the very sites the inventory marked "safe / module semantics intended", and a
second reachable shape (message-only sibling) shows the module-keyed
`exists()` gates — never inventoried — still misroute codegen. The deliverable
should not be described as closing the module-key defect class; it closes one
axis of it.
